WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.201
Inside a developing zebrafish, the heart contracts and

00:00:04.201 --> 00:00:07.582
blood circulates across structures small enough to

00:00:07.582 --> 00:00:09.679
challenge ordinary observation.

00:00:14.342 --> 00:00:17.998
Fast video microscopy captures change over time. The

00:00:17.998 --> 00:00:18.279
task

00:00:18.279 --> 00:00:21.457
becomes extracting meaningful cardiac and flow

00:00:21.457 --> 00:00:23.598
measurements from those frames.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:31.550
The published work examines cardiac function and

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.207
blood-flow hemodynamics, rather than treating a single

00:00:35.207 --> 00:00:38.187
attractive frame as quantitative physiology.

00:00:43.175 --> 00:00:47.166
Frame rate, optical scale and analysis assumptions can

00:00:47.166 --> 00:00:50.713
constrain an estimated velocity or physiological

00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:51.452
parameter.

00:00:55.758 --> 00:00:59.348
Vessels change shape while blood moves. Interpretation

00:00:59.348 --> 00:01:02.806
has to respect both the organism and the measurement

00:01:02.806 --> 00:01:03.470
technique.

00:01:07.508 --> 00:01:11.681
The 2020 Micron study explores the possibilities of

00:01:11.681 --> 00:01:15.936
high-speed video microscopy. Its original images and

00:01:15.936 --> 00:01:18.964
analytic details remain the evidence.
